{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering policy that removed penalties for small amounts of controlled substances will come to an end this weekend. Under the fresh legislation, HB4002, individuals found with illegal narcotics—like fentanyl, heroin, or meth—will now face legal consequences unless they opt for mandatory assistance. This revised approach lets authorities either press charges or steer users toward recovery programs, involving counseling and participation in diversion initiatives to dodge penalties. Minor personal possession will be classified as a misdemeanor, carrying up to six months behind bars.\n\nThe new rules emphasize stricter enforcement against public drug use, while also toughening penalties for dealers operating near public spaces such as parks. Counties are urged but not required to establish treatment options to reroute offenders from legal trouble into care for addiction and mental health. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor solutions to their capacities, whereas critics warn it could lead to inconsistent policies across the state.\n\nPortland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted strict limits for diversion, excluding those with prior offenses, outstanding warrants, or violent histories—or those in unstable health. Eligible cases trigger a call to a county hotline, where officials assess suitability and dispatch mental health teams with a 30-minute window to engage. Day noted officers cannot delay arrests if support arrives too late, reinforcing the shift toward immediate action over prolonged intervention.\n\n***P.P.S.\nWhile conversations about drug policy dominate headlines, everyday routines—like grocery runs or weekend outings—often reflect broader societal shifts. For many, the **age** at which habits form, such as developing a taste for certain foods, mirrors how communities adapt to change. Even casual choices, like selecting a snack during **shopping** trips, can subtly influence perceptions of normalcy. Meanwhile, debates over language, like the curiosity behind why some words roll off the **tongue** effortlessly while others feel foreign, hint at deeper cultural divides. Just as policies reshape behavior, so too do small, daily acts contribute to the evolving fabric of a **country**.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -8.95221673999913e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.0907151997089386}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.7902781963348389}, {"token": " policy", "logprob": -2.7318029403686523}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -1.8731141090393066}, {"token": " removed", "logprob": -0.6976461410522461}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.45364946126937866}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.00017414960893802345}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9622997641563416}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.6225395202636719}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.566918844124302e-05}, {"token": " controlled", "logprob": -1.181594729423523}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0037054228596389294}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.636984646320343}, {"token": " come", "logprob": -3.301011800765991}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.2053474822314456e-05}, {"token": " an", "logprob": -0.0012072187382727861}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.00034338299883529544}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.2853907346725464}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.18227680027484894}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.7142653465270996}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -1.3216891288757324}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.7986451983451843}, {"token": " fresh", "logprob": -4.8529815673828125}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.5851195454597473}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.5475864410400391}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -1.0762913227081299}, {"token": "4", "log